   "year": 1998,
   "number": 21,
   "url": "https://cemc.uwaterloo.ca/sites/default/files/documents/1998/1998CayleyContest.pdf"
  },
  "grade": "10",
  "points": 8,
  "timeMinutes": null,
  "type": "mc",
  "prompt": "When Betty substitutes $x=1$ into the expression $ax^3-2x+c$ its value is $-5$. When she substitutes $x=4$ the expression has value 52. One value of $x$ that makes the expression equal to zero is",
  "image": null,
  "choices": [
   {
    "key": "A",
    "text": "2"
   },
   {
    "key": "B",
    "text": "$\\frac52$"
   },
   {
    "key": "C",
    "text": "3"
   },
   {
    "key": "D",
    "text": "$\\frac72$"
   },
   {
    "key": "E",
    "text": "4"
   }
  ],
  "answer": "A",
  "acceptedAnswers": null,
  "answerUnit": null,
  "solutions": [
   {
    "title": "Find $a$ and $c$",
    "body": "$x=1$: $a-2+c=-5$, so $a+c=-3$. $x=4$: $64a-8+c=52$, so $64a+c=60$. Subtracting, $63a=63$, so $a=1$ and $c=-4$. The expression is $x^3-2x-4$, and $x=2$ gives $8-4-4=0$.\n\n**Answer: (A) 2**"
   },
   {
    "title": "Factor",
    "body": "With $a=1$, $c=-4$: $x^3-2x-4=(x-2)(x^2+2x+2)$, and $x^2+2x+2=(x+1)^2+1>0$, so $x=2$ is the only real zero. Checking the other choices, e.g. $x=3$ gives $17\\ne0$.\n\n**Answer: (A) 2**"
   }
  ],
  "topics": [
   "polynomials",
   "algebra"
  ],
  "difficulty": 3,
  "duplicateOf": null,
  "reviewNote": null
 },
 {
  "id": "cemc-cayley-1998-22",
  "bank": "cemc",
  "source": {
